WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:11.695
<v Maya>Imagine you're navigating this massive pitch black maze, right? Right. You're looking for a specific prize hidden somewhere in there, but you only have a flashlight and the batteries are dying fast.</v>

2
00:00:11.955 --> 00:00:12.976
<v Daniel>Not an ideal situation.</v>

3
00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:21.079
<v Maya>Right. Every time you turn down a corridor, your flashlight dims just a little bit more. If you check too many empty hallways, you're going to be left completely in the dark.</v>

4
00:00:21.239 --> 00:00:26.661
<v Daniel>Yeah. And that is exactly what experimental discovery feels like when you're constrained by time and money.</v>

5
00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:38.169
<v Maya>Exactly. Whether you're hunting for, you know, a new drug or optimizing a chemical reaction or designing some novel material, you are always facing that same really expensive question.</v>

6
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.352
<v Daniel>Which is which path do we eliminate next?</v>

7
00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:47.957
<v Maya>Yes. Because in the real world of scientific research, those flashlight batteries are million dollar budgets.</v>

8
00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:56.163
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely. And like months of laboratory labor, you can't just wander around hoping to bump into a breakthrough. You need a highly disciplined system.</v>

9
00:00:56.771 --> 00:01:04.525
<v Maya>Which brings us to our mission for today's deep dive. We are unpacking a system that completely flips the script on how we navigate that maze.</v>

10
00:01:04.925 --> 00:01:08.552
<v Daniel>We're looking at the bleeding edge of AI and scientific discovery today.</v>

11
00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:17.225
<v Maya>We are. We've got a stack of six research papers in front of us, but we're really going to shine the spotlight on one absolute game changer.</v>

12
00:01:17.345 --> 00:01:18.426
<v Daniel>Yeah, the Gollum paper.</v>

13
00:01:18.506 --> 00:01:28.475
<v Maya>Right. It's titled Large Language Models as Uncertainty Calibrated Optimizers for Experimental Discovery, published by Bojana Rankovic, Ryan Reis Griffiths, and Philippe Schwaller.</v>

14
00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:31.158
<v Daniel>In Nature Machine Intelligence on August 28, 2026.</v>

15
00:01:32.839 --> 00:01:39.965
<v Maya>And what's so fascinating here is it tackles this massive elephant in the room with large language models, right? Like their fatal flaw.</v>

16
00:01:39.985 --> 00:01:47.632
<v Daniel>Their overconfidence. Yeah. Foundation models, they hallucinate. They sound incredibly sure of themselves while being completely dangerously wrong.</v>

17
00:01:47.872 --> 00:02:01.083
<v Maya>And normally scientists try to just suppress that or they disqualify LLMs from high stakes decisions entirely. But this new framework, GOLLUM, which stands for Gaussian Process Optimized LLMs, it does something wild.</v>

