00:02:01.324 --> 00:02:07.955
<v Daniel>It really does. It doesn't just try to put a filter over the hallucination. It actively uses that exact flaw as a training signal.</v>

19
00:02:08.307 --> 00:02:18.969
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this because to understand why Gollum is such a profound breakthrough, we kind of need to look at the two broken compasses scientists have been using in that maze.</v>

20
00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:23.750
<v Daniel>Right, so on one side you have traditional Bayesian optimization or BO.</v>

21
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.911
<v Maya>Which is like the gold standard, right?</v>

22
00:02:25.931 --> 00:02:39.494
<v Daniel>It is the absolute gold standard for experimental discovery because it handles uncertainty beautifully. It runs all this math to balance exploration. So... Checking unknown areas of the maze with exploitation.</v>

23
00:02:39.714 --> 00:02:42.095
<v Maya>Meaning focusing on areas we already know are decent.</v>

24
00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:49.797
<v Daniel>Exactly. But Bayesian optimization has a massive bottleneck. It requires these hand-built, domain-specific representations.</v>

25
00:02:50.037 --> 00:02:57.499
<v Maya>Let me translate that for anyone who hasn't worked in a chemistry lab. If you want a computer to understand a chemical reaction, you usually have to translate it into a fingerprint.</v>

26
