00:05:06.301 --> 00:05:20.857
<v Maya>Wait, hold on. Gaussian process marginal likelihood? That sounds like, I mean, a grad school hazing term. Let's ground that in an analogy so we don't lose anyone. Fair enough. Imagine you're pairing up a brilliant but highly erratic polymath, that's our language model,</v>

47
00:05:21.358 --> 00:05:24.482
<v Maya>with a strict probability-obsessed actuary.</v>

48
00:05:24.502 --> 00:05:26.224
<v Daniel>Okay, the Gaussian process.</v>

49
00:05:26.505 --> 00:05:31.652
<v Maya>Right. Left alone, the polymath just spouts out a million creative ideas, half of which are nonsense.</v>

50
00:05:32.137 --> 00:05:47.046
<v Daniel>And the actuary is there to calculate risk. So in the Golem framework, the actuary uses probabilistic gradients to literally train the polymath. Wow. That marginal likelihood you just mentioned, it's essentially the actuary's rulebook. It's a</v>

