00:07:37.698 --> 00:07:52.549
<v Daniel>The Elad ratio essentially measures how smooth or chaotic that terrain is. If the ratio is high, the landscape is like a smooth rolling hill. Similar chemical experiments are clustered closely together, creating a predictable terrain where the algorithm</v>

67
00:07:52.569 --> 00:08:04.418
<v Daniel>can easily guess where the highest point is. Got it. But if the ratio is low, the landscape is a jagged, spiked cliffside. It's chaotic. A tiny step in one direction could drop you off a cliff.</v>

68
00:08:04.673 --> 00:08:09.697
<v Maya>So a high ratio means a safe, predictable map. A low ratio means a dangerous, broken map.</v>

69
00:08:09.817 --> 00:08:17.723
<v Daniel>Exactly. And across 14 different representations, that old ratio perfectly predicted optimization performance with a 0.92 correlation.</v>

70
00:08:17.883 --> 00:08:20.104
<v Maya>A 0.92 correlation is staggering.</v>

71
00:08:20.184 --> 00:08:35.173
<v Daniel>It really is. It's actually a better predictor of success than the surrogate model's raw predictive accuracy. Wait, really? Yeah. It works through this phenomenon called implicit contrastive learning. The language model is being fine-tuned by the Gaussian process's marginal likelihood</v>

