148
00:18:23.682 --> 00:18:24.422
<v Maya>Oh, interesting.</v>

149
00:18:24.462 --> 00:18:36.068
<v Daniel>Yeah, that underlying philosophy isn't actually that far from what Gollum is doing. Gollum simply argues you need the Gaussian process to discipline that exploration when the math gets continuous and messy.</v>

150
00:18:36.498 --> 00:18:44.944
<v Maya>That makes total sense. Now, with any scientific breakthrough, we have to look at the honest limitations before we just, you know, declare total victory.</v>

151
00:18:45.204 --> 00:18:49.807
<v Daniel>Yeah, Gollum is not magic. The authors are very transparent about where this currently falls short.</v>

152
00:18:50.227 --> 00:18:51.388
<v Maya>Let's bring it back down to Earth.</v>

153
00:18:51.929 --> 00:19:00.915
<v Daniel>First is computational scaling. Standard Gaussian processes scale cutically. As you add more data points to the history, the compute required skyrockets.</v>

154
00:19:01.375 --> 00:19:05.800
<v Maya>So if you want to run a laboratory campaign with thousands of experiments rather than 50,</v>

155
00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:11.246
<v Daniel>this exact setup will choke. You would need to introduce mathematical approximations to handle that scale.</v>

156
00:19:11.621 --> 00:19:19.604
<v Maya>Got it. And there's also the limitation of using text to describe physical reality, right? We're shoehorning 3D chemistry into English strings.</v>

157
00:19:19.784 --> 00:19:34.650
<v Daniel>Exactly. If you're working in a design space that lacks a clean linguistic structure like trying to describe complex 3D protein conformations or highly intricate crystal structures, a text interface might fundamentally be the wrong</v>

158
00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:35.470
<v Daniel>tool for the job.</v>

159
00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:39.873
<v Maya>Right. You can't just describe a folding protein in a paragraph and expect perfect geometry.</v>

160
00:19:39.913 --> 00:19:40.634
<v Daniel>You really can't.</v>

161
00:19:40.834 --> 00:19:41.014
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

162
00:19:41.294 --> 00:19:45.878
<v Daniel>And finally, we have to acknowledge that all 23 benchmarks in this paper are retrospective.</v>

163
00:19:46.238 --> 00:19:50.221
<v Maya>Meaning they're fully enumerated historical data sets where the answers are already known.</v>

164
00:19:50.730 --> 00:20:05.179
<v Daniel>Right. They are not live, messy, real-time laboratory campaigns where, you know, the ambient humidity in the room might suddenly ruin a reaction. Validating this in a closed-loop, self-scribing laboratory is the necessary next step to see if it actually survives</v>

165
00:20:05.259 --> 00:20:06.760
<v Daniel>contact with physical reality.</v>

166
00:20:07.062 --> 00:20:10.226
<v Maya>Even with those limits, though, the paradigm shift here is undeniable.</v>

167
00:20:10.306 --> 00:20:10.847
<v Daniel>Oh, completely.</v>

168
00:20:11.027 --> 00:20:24.025
<v Maya>The closing line of the paper is the ultimate thesis worth landing on. The authors state that Gollum provides a path to specializing foundation models, not through pumping them with more data, but through richer, uncertainty-guided information.</v>

169
00:20:24.561 --> 00:20:28.564
<v Daniel>It challenges the entire scale is all you need philosophy in AI.</v>

170
00:20:28.704 --> 00:20:29.004
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

171
00:20:29.145 --> 00:20:41.414
<v Daniel>For years, the assumption has been that to make an AI better at a specific hard task, you just need to feed it a billion more tokens of specialized text. Gollum proves that calibration beats raw data hoarding.</v>

172
00:20:41.592 --> 00:20:56.321
<v Maya>Which leaves you with something pretty profound to chew on. If calibrated uncertainty rather than more data is what truly specializes a foundation model for a high stakes domain like experimental chemistry, what else does that recipe apply to? Exactly. Think about it.</v>

173
00:20:56.441 --> 00:21:11.373
<v Maya>What other fields rely on massive amounts of text but require absolute mathematically calibrated confidence before pulling the trigger? Could we use this exact deep kernel learning architecture to navigate the maze of medical diagnostics? pairing messy patient histories with strict</v>

174
00:21:11.493 --> 00:21:12.655
<v Maya>probabilistic outcomes?</v>

175
00:21:13.156 --> 00:21:25.630
<v Daniel>Or could it revolutionize high-frequency financial markets or complex legal policy? Any domain where being confidently wrong is catastrophic could theoretically benefit from this exact architecture.</v>

176
00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:40.699
<v Maya>The battery in the flashlight is still limited. We still have limited time, limited money, and limited resources to solve the world's biggest problems. But if we can train our models to map the walls of their own failures, maybe we finally found a way to stop checking the empty hallways. Thanks for joining</v>

177
00:21:40.719 --> 00:21:42.402
<v Maya>us on this deep dive. Catch you next time.</v>
